WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.890
Continuous antimicrobial violet-blue light.

00:00:02.980 --> 00:00:07.210
Low-level illumination over cows, calves, and water troughs.

00:00:07.320 --> 00:00:09.950
A selected band in the visible violet-blue spectrum —

00:00:10.000 --> 00:00:13.270
always on, so the antimicrobial dose builds while animals are present.

00:00:14.340 --> 00:00:16.950
How this light inactivates bacteria.

00:00:17.138 --> 00:00:21.850
First, bacteria contain natural photosensitizers — porphyrins and flavins.

00:00:22.058 --> 00:00:25.850
Second, selected violet-blue wavelengths are absorbed inside the cell.

00:00:26.058 --> 00:00:29.548
Third, with oxygen present, reactive oxygen species form.

00:00:29.598 --> 00:00:31.930
Fourth, those reactive oxygen species oxidize membranes,

00:00:31.980 --> 00:00:35.026
proteins, and DNA, and the cells are inactivated.

00:00:35.076 --> 00:00:40.550
This is gradual oxidative damage from continuous low-level light — not a flash zap.

00:00:41.600 --> 00:00:43.110
Scientific takeaway.

00:00:43.900 --> 00:00:47.490
Continuous low-level violet-blue light in a selected spectral band.

00:00:47.758 --> 00:00:52.590
Natural bacterial photosensitizers absorb that light and generate reactive oxygen species.

00:00:53.080 --> 00:00:54.750
Those species oxidize membranes, proteins, and DNA until cells fail.

00:00:55.540 --> 00:00:58.890
It needs oxygen, and efficacy depends on dose and bacterial species.

00:00:59.700 --> 00:01:03.530
Always-on barn fixtures deliver a cumulative antimicrobial dose.

00:01:04.960 --> 00:01:06.810
White Leaf Technology Group.

00:01:07.580 --> 00:01:09.410
Bringing light to the dairy industry.

00:01:10.000 --> 00:01:12.332
